export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"partcraft","addon":"magnetics","name":"Magnetics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"magnetics-start","scope":"addon:magnetics","title":"Magnetics: getting started","summary":"Set up a flat magnet study in PartCraft (iron, permanent magnets, coils and the air round them), solve it, and read the flux density and the force on a body.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["magnet","magnetics","magnetostatic","electromagnet","solenoid","coil","permanent magnet","flux","flux density","tesla","force","air gap","b-h curve","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"the-examples","text":"The examples","level":2},{"id":"your-sketches","text":"Your sketches","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESwitch the add-on on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003EMagnetics\u003C/strong\u003E tab in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAn example device\u003C/span\u003E to start from a ready-made cross-section, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis part&#039;s sketches\u003C/span\u003E to use your own drawing. With your sketches, each closed profile is one body; bodies that touch go in different sketches, and all the sketches must lie on one plane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBodies\u003C/span\u003E say what each body is: air, iron (a relative permeability μr, or your own B–H curve), a permanent magnet (Br in tesla and the direction it points, in degrees from +x) or a coil (the total current through its cross-section: turns × amperes; the sign says which way it runs). Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForce on it\u003C/span\u003E for each body whose force you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAir box and depth\u003C/span\u003E choose how much air goes round the bodies, the depth of the device out of the section (forces are scaled by it) and the mesh.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolve\u003C/span\u003E. The field picture, the largest |B|, each body&#039;s mean and largest |B|, the forces and the flux lines appear under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResults\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOn the part\u003C/span\u003E click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow on the part\u003C/span\u003E: the result goes into the 3D view as display bodies. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the results\u003C/span\u003E choose a file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-examples\"\u003EThe examples\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFive devices you can change by size:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EC-core electromagnet\u003C/strong\u003E: an iron C with an air gap and a coil round its back leg. The gap field is compared with the magnetic circuit, μ0·NI / (gap + iron path / μr).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMagnet in a closed iron frame\u003C/strong\u003E: compared with the magnetic circuit, Br·(lm/μrm) / (lm/μrm + L/μr).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHolding magnet with a keeper\u003C/strong\u003E: the pull on the keeper. There is no hand formula for this one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne round conductor\u003C/strong\u003E: compared with B = μ0·I / 2πr.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo parallel conductors\u003C/strong\u003E: the force between them, compared with μ0·I² / 2πd per metre.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere an example has a hand formula, the result line says the hand value, the computed value and how far apart they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-sketches\"\u003EYour sketches\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw each body as a closed profile: a rectangle, a circle, any closed outline. A magnet inside an iron yoke can share a sketch with the yoke when it sits inside it; a body that only touches another goes in a sketch of its own. Profiles that cross are refused, with the reason.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Switch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons. Open the Magnetics tab in the right panel. Under Model choose An example device to start from a ready-made cross-section, or This part's sketches to use your own drawing. With your sketches, each closed profile is one body; bodies that touch go in different sketches, and all the sketches must lie on one plane. Under Bodies say what each body is: air, iron (a relative permeability μr, or your own B–H curve), a permanent magnet (Br in tesla and the direction it points, in degrees from +x) or a coil (the total current through its cross-section: turns × amperes; the sign says which way it runs). Tick Force on it for each body whose force you want. Under Air box and depth choose how much air goes round the bodies, the depth of the device out of the section (forces are scaled by it) and the mesh. Click Solve. The field picture, the largest |B|, each body's mean and largest |B|, the forces and the flux lines appear under Results. Under On the part click Show on the part: the result goes into the 3D view as display bodies. Under Save the results choose a file. The examples Five devices you can change by size: C-core electromagnet: an iron C with an air gap and a coil round its back leg. The gap field is compared with the magnetic circuit, μ0·NI / (gap + iron path / μr). Magnet in a closed iron frame: compared with the magnetic circuit, Br·(lm/μrm) / (lm/μrm + L/μr). Holding magnet with a keeper: the pull on the keeper. There is no hand formula for this one. One round conductor: compared with B = μ0·I / 2πr. Two parallel conductors: the force between them, compared with μ0·I² / 2πd per metre. Where an example has a hand formula, the result line says the hand value, the computed value and how far apart they are. Your sketches Draw each body as a closed profile: a rectangle, a circle, any closed outline. A magnet inside an iron yoke can share a sketch with the yoke when it sits inside it; a body that only touches another goes in a sketch of its own. Profiles that cross are refused, with the reason."},{"slug":"magnetics-results","scope":"addon:magnetics","title":"Magnetics: reading and saving the results","summary":"What the field picture, the flux lines, the forces and the hand comparison mean, how the result is shown on the part, and the files you can save.","section":"tasks","order":2,"keywords":["flux lines","flux density","maxwell stress","force","torque","vtk","paraview","csv","svg","display bodies","results","export"],"headings":[{"id":"the-field-and-the-flux-lines","text":"The field and the flux lines","level":2},{"id":"forces-and-torque","text":"Forces and torque","level":2},{"id":"on-the-part","text":"On the part","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-field-and-the-flux-lines\"\u003EThe field and the flux lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture colors each triangle by |B|, from dark blue (0) to red. Red means the top of the scale or more: the top is the |B| that 99 percent of the triangles stay under, so one sharp iron corner does not paint the rest of the model dark. The largest |B| is given in words beside it. The white lines are flux lines: in a flat model they are the lines of equal vector potential A, and between two neighboring lines the same flux flows. The results say how much, for the depth you gave.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"forces-and-torque\"\u003EForces and torque\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe force on a body is the Maxwell stress added up round a box in the air around it. The box sits in the middle of the nearest air gap. The torque is about the body&#039;s middle. The result is per metre of depth times the depth you gave.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe force depends on the mesh near the body, most of all through a thin gap. Run the study with the Normal mesh and again with Fine. If the two forces differ a lot, the force is not settled. The study warns when a force box runs within three triangles of its body.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-the-part\"\u003EOn the part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow on the part\u003C/span\u003E puts the result in the 3D view, in the study&#039;s plane: the bodies as see-through blocks out to the depth, the field and the flux lines near them (half the model&#039;s size round them) on their far face, and a green arrow for each force. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAir box\u003C/span\u003E to also draw the outline of the whole air box. Tick what you want shown. The result is placed in one undo step, saved with the part and taken off with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake off the part\u003C/span\u003E or Undo. These are display bodies only: they have no mass and are not in STL or STEP. When a fine mesh would need more triangles than the viewport allows, the bodies are left out and the message says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sketch on a face or a reference plane is shown on the front plane, at the sketch&#039;s own coordinates. The panel says when this happens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESummary (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E: the largest |B|, each body&#039;s area, mean and largest |B|, the forces and torques, and the hand comparison.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EField per triangle (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E: position, body, Bx, By and |B| of every triangle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EField (VTK)\u003C/strong\u003E: the mesh with B per triangle and A per point, for ParaView or VisIt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPicture (SVG)\u003C/strong\u003E: the field, the flux lines and the outlines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe study (JSON)\u003C/strong\u003E: what you set up, to keep or share.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe summary and the VTK file are also in File › Export once the study is solved.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The field and the flux lines The picture colors each triangle by |B|, from dark blue (0) to red. Red means the top of the scale or more: the top is the |B| that 99 percent of the triangles stay under, so one sharp iron corner does not paint the rest of the model dark. The largest |B| is given in words beside it. The white lines are flux lines: in a flat model they are the lines of equal vector potential A, and between two neighboring lines the same flux flows. The results say how much, for the depth you gave. Forces and torque The force on a body is the Maxwell stress added up round a box in the air around it. The box sits in the middle of the nearest air gap. The torque is about the body's middle. The result is per metre of depth times the depth you gave. The force depends on the mesh near the body, most of all through a thin gap. Run the study with the Normal mesh and again with Fine. If the two forces differ a lot, the force is not settled. The study warns when a force box runs within three triangles of its body. On the part Show on the part puts the result in the 3D view, in the study's plane: the bodies as see-through blocks out to the depth, the field and the flux lines near them (half the model's size round them) on their far face, and a green arrow for each force. Tick Air box to also draw the outline of the whole air box. Tick what you want shown. The result is placed in one undo step, saved with the part and taken off with Take off the part or Undo. These are display bodies only: they have no mass and are not in STL or STEP. When a fine mesh would need more triangles than the viewport allows, the bodies are left out and the message says so. A sketch on a face or a reference plane is shown on the front plane, at the sketch's own coordinates. The panel says when this happens. Files Summary (CSV): the largest |B|, each body's area, mean and largest |B|, the forces and torques, and the hand comparison. Field per triangle (CSV): position, body, Bx, By and |B| of every triangle. Field (VTK): the mesh with B per triangle and A per point, for ParaView or VisIt. Picture (SVG): the field, the flux lines and the outlines. The study (JSON): what you set up, to keep or share. The summary and the VTK file are also in File › Export once the study is solved."},{"slug":"magnetics-limits","scope":"addon:magnetics","title":"Magnetics: limits and what is not built yet","summary":"What the magnet solver does and does not do, how it was checked, and what to use it for.","section":"reference","order":90,"keywords":["limits","accuracy","2-d","3-d","axisymmetric","eddy currents","hysteresis","demagnetization","saturation","closed-form","verification","not built"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"how-it-was-checked","text":"How it was checked","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA flat (2-D) magnetostatic solver for long devices: the cross-section is solved with the current running in and out of the section, and the answer is per metre of depth. It uses linear triangles for the vector potential and Newton&#039;s method for iron that saturates. The force comes from the Maxwell stress. It is PartCraft&#039;s own solver, the same one as Sketch › Magnet study; this add-on gives it a panel, examples, the result on the part and files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-it-was-checked\"\u003EHow it was checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAgainst closed-form answers, in the add-on&#039;s own tests:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe field of a round conductor at twice its radius, within 1 percent of μ0·I / 2πr, and inside the conductor within 5 percent;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe flux between two flux lines of that conductor, within 5 percent of μ0·I/2π · ln(r2/r1);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea magnet in a closed iron frame, within 1 percent of the magnetic circuit;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe force between two parallel conductors, within 4 percent of μ0·I² / 2πd with a box four sizes wide. With a box one size wide it is about 38 percent low, because the box edge acts as a wall, and the result line flags it;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe gap field of the C-core example, within 5 percent of the magnetic circuit. The circuit leaves out fringing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe solver itself is also tested in PartCraft&#039;s own suite against a line current, a saturating slab and a magnet across its axis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo axisymmetric (round) models and no 3-D. A short device, or one with ends that matter, is not modelled well.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESteady fields only: no eddy currents, no AC, no hysteresis, no motion.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA magnet follows a straight recoil line. There is no demagnetization knee, so a magnet driven past its knee is not caught.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIron is linear unless you give a B–H curve. The study warns when linear iron goes past 2.1 T.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo material library: every number is yours or an example (iron μr 2000, magnet 1.2 T), not a data sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe solve runs in a background worker on the page. A Fine mesh of a detailed model can take a minute.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is an analysis aid for a first estimate. It is not a design approval.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is A flat (2-D) magnetostatic solver for long devices: the cross-section is solved with the current running in and out of the section, and the answer is per metre of depth. It uses linear triangles for the vector potential and Newton's method for iron that saturates. The force comes from the Maxwell stress. It is PartCraft's own solver, the same one as Sketch › Magnet study; this add-on gives it a panel, examples, the result on the part and files. How it was checked Against closed-form answers, in the add-on's own tests: the field of a round conductor at twice its radius, within 1 percent of μ0·I / 2πr, and inside the conductor within 5 percent; the flux between two flux lines of that conductor, within 5 percent of μ0·I/2π · ln(r2/r1); a magnet in a closed iron frame, within 1 percent of the magnetic circuit; the force between two parallel conductors, within 4 percent of μ0·I² / 2πd with a box four sizes wide. With a box one size wide it is about 38 percent low, because the box edge acts as a wall, and the result line flags it; the gap field of the C-core example, within 5 percent of the magnetic circuit. The circuit leaves out fringing. The solver itself is also tested in PartCraft's own suite against a line current, a saturating slab and a magnet across its axis. Not built yet No axisymmetric (round) models and no 3-D. A short device, or one with ends that matter, is not modelled well. Steady fields only: no eddy currents, no AC, no hysteresis, no motion. A magnet follows a straight recoil line. There is no demagnetization knee, so a magnet driven past its knee is not caught. Iron is linear unless you give a B–H curve. The study warns when linear iron goes past 2.1 T. No material library: every number is yours or an example (iron μr 2000, magnet 1.2 T), not a data sheet. The solve runs in a background worker on the page. A Fine mesh of a detailed model can take a minute. This is an analysis aid for a first estimate. It is not a design approval."}]};
